#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/id #uuid "4b092f86-cf53-51b4-9f79-b43d58c8b2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5563" :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthonormal:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given set as $\\{\\mathbf{w}_1,\\mathbf{w}_2\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe set $W$ is orthonormal if the following conditions hold:\n\n$$\n{w_{1} \\cdot w_{1} = 1; w_{2} \\cdot w_{2} = 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our set:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\cdot w_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} - \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} = 0 & ✓ \\\\ w_{1} \\cdot w_{1}|= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = 1 & ✓ \\\\ w_{2} \\cdot w_{2}|= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} = 16 + 16 = 32 \\ne 1 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are orthogonal, and $\\mathbf w_1$ is a unit vector, but $\\mathbf w_2$ is *not* a unit vector.\n\nTherefore, ${W}$ is *not* an orthonormal set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "af1ca39e-5cb7-5a93-b88f-0ff86a563bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5564" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T$, extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2$. To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$, we need to find a third vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n{u_{1} \\cdot x = 0; u_{2} \\cdot x = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). This means $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_3=0$. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-x_{1} - x_{2} + 2(0) = 0⟹x_{1} =-x_{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSetting $x_2=1$, we obtain the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "7ef88417-f089-51ea-9fac-dccef5825016" :question/math-academy-id "e-7114" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T$, extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2$. To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$, we need to find a third vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\ &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{u_{1} \\cdot x = 0; u_{2} \\cdot x = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=\\sqrt{2} \\cdot R_{1} \\\\ R_{2}:=\\sqrt{6} \\cdot R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). This means $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3$, and from the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_3$.\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{3} \\\\ -x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_3=1$, we obtain the vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2+1^2} = \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we normalize $\\mathbf x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/id #uuid "8a41b0e9-c029-5175-80c9-5d9c6c1dd5f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7257" :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthogonal:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "If we denote the vectors in our set ${X}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\mathbf{x}_3 \\}$, we can check that ${X}$ is orthogonal by showing that\n\n$$\n{x_{1} \\cdot x_{2} = 0; x_{1} \\cdot x_{3} = 0; x_{2} \\cdot x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\cdot x_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = 2 \\cdot (-1) + 0 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 2 = 0 & ✓ \\\\ x_{1} \\cdot x_{3}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = 2 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\cdot (-2) + 1 \\cdot 0 = 2 \\ne 0 & \\cdot \\\\ x_{2} \\cdot x_{3}|= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = (-1) \\cdot 1 + 0 \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot 0 =-1 \\ne 0 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo ${X}$ is *not* an orthogonal set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0af6271-c27b-541e-9dde-5f993f186c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131283} {:answer/id #uuid "f856ced4-de4a-541f-80b6-270c8d208a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131284} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6b0ea1-b95e-5f27-b3e0-640034849bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131285} {:answer/id #uuid "03476a76-f102-5586-b40b-89089d81f861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131286} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd5c0